Introduction
Our AI assistant can hold a conversation and place trades, but it is blind. It knows only what the user types into the prompt, so when a trader asks what it makes of the chart in front of them, or how their open trades are doing, the assistant has nothing to look at. It cannot see the price action on the screen or read the account state: open positions, trade history, indicator values, and chart objects. The model can reason only over what is typed into text; anything omitted effectively does not exist for it. That gap between what the trader sees and what the assistant knows is what keeps it a chat box rather than a co-pilot. This article is for
MetaQuotes Language 5
(MQL5) developers building AI interfaces who want their assistant to reason from the live chart and account rather than typed descriptions alone.
In our
previous article (Part 11)
, we made the interface fast, repainting only what changed under rapid interaction. In this article, we narrow that gap with two new abilities: vision and tool access. The assistant can capture the chart as a screenshot and attach it to a message, viewing it in the panel, and it can call tools that read selected account and market data on demand. We will cover the following topics:
Understanding Vision Input and Tool Calling
Sending Chart Vision to the Model
Giving the Model Market Tools
Visualization
Conclusion
By the end of this article, you will be able to:
Capture the current chart as a screenshot and attach it to a message, then view it in the panel through a thumbnail and a full-image viewer.
Send that visual content to the model so it reasons from the chart it can see rather than a typed description alone.
Give the model a set of tools it can call on its own to read live positions, trade history, indicator values, chart objects, and the economic calendar.
Understanding Vision Input and Tool Calling
Until now, the assistant has lived entirely in text. A message goes up as words, a reply comes back as words, and anything about the chart or the account has to be typed out by hand before the model can reason about it. The two abilities we add in this article break that limit from opposite ends: vision lets the model take in a picture, and tool calling lets the model reach out for data it does not have. One widens what we can send; the other widens what the model can fetch.
Vision input rests on the fact that a modern model can accept an image alongside text in the same message. We capture the chart as a screenshot, encode it as base64, and attach it to the message as image content. The model then receives the chart as an image and can interpret the candles, levels, and drawn objects visible in it, though it is reading pixels rather than chart data and can misjudge fine detail. Anything drawn on the chart is captured with it, so a chart we have marked up becomes something the assistant can read rather than guess at. The illustration below traces this path from the chart to the model.
Tool calling flips the direction of the exchange. Instead of us pushing everything the model might need into the prompt, we hand the model a menu of tools it can request, each described by a name, a purpose, and the inputs it accepts. When the model decides it needs live data to answer, it does not reply with prose; it replies with a call, naming the tool and its arguments. Our program runs that call against the terminal, returns the result, and lets the model continue with the answer in hand. The illustration below shows this round trip.
In practice, they work best together. Vision provides the market's structure at a glance, while tools provide the precise figures: position sizes, indicator readings, and upcoming calendar events. We decide what the model may see by choosing what to capture and attach, and we decide what it may fetch by choosing which tools to expose and what each one is allowed to read. The model supplies the reasoning; we supply the eyes and the reach, and keep control of both.
Sending Chart Vision to the Model
Sight comes in three steps: capture a clean chart image, encode it into something a request can carry, and stage it to travel with the next message. We start where the pixels turn into a payload: the encoder.
Encoding the Image
A request can only carry text, so the raw image bytes have to become
base64
before anything else.
string
Ai12_Base64Encode(
const
uchar
&data[])
  {
uchar
key[];
uchar
out[];
int
n =
CryptEncode
(
CRYPT_BASE64
, data, key, out);
if
(n <=
0
)
return
""
;
string
s =
CharArrayToString
(out,
0
, n,
CP_UTF8
);
StringReplace
(s,
"\r"
,
""
);
StringReplace
(s,
"\n"
,
""
);
return
s;
  }
Here, inside the "Ai12_Base64Encode" function, we run the image bytes through the
CryptEncode
call in base64 mode and strip any line breaks so the result is one clean string safe to drop into
JavaScript Object Notation
(JSON). We treat this as the bridge between binary pixels and a text payload, and we pass every image we send across it. With the encoder ready, we can capture the chart itself and feed it in.
Capturing the Chart
The screenshot has to be of the clean chart, not the panel sitting over it, and it needs to come back at the chart's own pixel size, held between 320 by 240 and 4096 by 4096.
