void
Ai_RegisterImgRect(
string
base,
int
x,
int
y,
int
w,
int
h,
int
paneTop,
int
paneBot)
  {
int
t =
MathMax
(y, paneTop);
int
b =
MathMin
(y + h, paneBot);
if
(b <= t)
return
;
// fully clipped away
int
n =
ArraySize
(g_ai_imgClickBase);
ArrayResize
(g_ai_imgClickBase, n +
1
);
ArrayResize
(g_ai_imgClickL, n +
1
);
ArrayResize
(g_ai_imgClickT, n +
1
);
ArrayResize
(g_ai_imgClickR, n +
1
);
ArrayResize
(g_ai_imgClickB, n +
1
);
   g_ai_imgClickBase[n] = base;
   g_ai_imgClickL[n] = x;
   g_ai_imgClickT[n] = t;
   g_ai_imgClickR[n] = x + w;
   g_ai_imgClickB[n] = b;
  }
Through the "Ai_BlitThumb" function, we scale the cached pixels into the target rectangle with nearest-neighbor sampling, clipping to the pane so a thumbnail never spills past the chat edges as it scrolls. Each time we draw one, we call the "Ai_RegisterImgRect" function to append its clipped on-screen rectangle to a set of parallel arrays we rebuild every chat render. Keeping this fresh hit-test list is what lets a click on a thumbnail find the right image, which we will use when the viewer opens.
The Compose Chip
Before an image is even sent, the staged attachment shows as a small chip above the prompt, with its own thumbnail and a remove badge.
